{"kind":"finding","job":2,"id":"F-2-1","file":"cli/src/cli/search.py","line":75,"summary":"The kind-formatting idiom `kind_val = result.get(\"kind\"); kind_display = kind_val if kind_val else \"-\"` is a byte-for-byte second copy of the same two lines already in cli/src/cli/list.py (lines 127-128: `kind_val = entry.get(\"kind\"); kind_display = kind_val if kind_val else \"-\"`). cli/src/cli/format.py exists specifically to hold display formatting `list` and `search` share (its docstring: \"Shared display formatting for CLI table output\", and format_priority is already the precedent for exactly this cross-command sharing). The job had the established extension point and did not use it, pasting the dash-fallback logic a second time instead of adding a `format_kind` helper alongside `format_priority`. The gate's automated duplication check flagged this exact block in job 2's first submission (`duplication(cli/src/cli/list.py:122-133 <-> cli/src/cli/search.py:101-112 (12 lines))`, journal.jsonl line 5) but the second submission's surrounding column reordering (inserting the Kind column between Priority and Score) shifted the line window enough that the detector no longer reports it (journal.jsonl line 11, no duplication failures) even though the semantic duplicate is unchanged in the code that landed.","severity":"low","ts":"2026-09-29T23:49:28.718Z"}
